clevis-zfs unlocks ZFS natively encrypted datasets at boot without
an operator, by the Tang protocol (Network-Bound Disk Encryption): the
dataset key is bound to one or several Tang servers (Shamir secret sharing,
threshold K of N) and recovered at boot as long as enough of them answer.
A stolen disk, away from that network, stays locked.

The rc.d script clevis_zfs runs after the network is up and unlocks,
then mounts, the datasets listed in clevis_zfs_datasets.
